    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Manichaeism was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, a prophet from Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, who from about 240 taught that the world is a mixture of light and darkness and that souls, sparks of light, can be freed through knowledge and discipline.</li><li>Organised into an elite of the Elect and a laity of Hearers, it spread west into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> was a Hearer, and east along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>Persecuted by Zoroastrian, Christian, Muslim and Chinese rulers alike, it disappeared by the early modern period, leaving its name to any world view that divides everything into good and evil.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A temple at Cao’an near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> still holds a stone image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> as the “Buddha of Light”, carved in 1339: the only Manichaean shrine in the world known to survive.</p></aside>
    </section>

