      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">216</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, near the Persian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 240</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> begins to preach his religion, after a revelation in his youth</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 274–277</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> dies in prison under the Sasanian king Bahram I</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 302</span><span class="rf-text">The Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> orders the persecution of the Manichaeans</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 373–382</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> is a Manichaean Hearer before his conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">762–763</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan">Bögü Qaghan</a> adopts Manichaeism, the faith of his empire for decades</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">843</span><span class="rf-text">The Tang emperor Wuzong bans Manichaeism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1339</span><span class="rf-text">The image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> as the Buddha of Light is carved at Cao’an near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a></span></li></ol>
