    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shapur-i.jpg" alt="Shapur I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shapour_I_statue.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown after Rawlinson, George, 1812-1902, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manichaeism was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, who was born in 216 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and soon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, near its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. His father had joined a community of Jewish-Christian baptists, the Elchasaites, and Mani grew up among them. According to Manichaean accounts, he received revelations from a heavenly twin, a divine counterpart, as a boy and again at about the age of twenty-four, and around 240 he broke with the baptists and began to preach a new religion.</p><p>Mani presented himself as the last in a line of prophets sent by God, after the Buddha in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Zoroaster in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the West, each of whose teachings, he held, had been corrupted by their followers. His message would be the final, complete revelation, written down by himself so that it could not be distorted. He called himself an apostle of Jesus Christ and the Paraclete, the helper promised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>.</p><p>Mani travelled to India and then returned to Persia, where he gained the favour of the new Sasanian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, to whom he dedicated a summary of his teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, the Shabuhragan. Under Shapur’s protection he sent missionaries east and west and organised a church. After Shapur’s death, however, the Zoroastrian priesthood, led by the high priest Kartir, turned the court against him; under King Bahram I Mani was imprisoned and died in chains, around 274 to 277. His followers remembered his death as a crucifixion and his passing as a release of his soul to the realm of light.</p><p>Mani’s religion drew on many sources: Jewish-Christian baptist traditions, the letters of Paul and the Gnostic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> of the 2nd and 3rd centuries, Zoroastrian dualism and Buddhist ideas of renunciation and rebirth. It was designed from the start as a universal faith, to be preached to every people in its own language and terms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of Manichaeism is a radical dualism. In the beginning there were two principles, uncreated and opposed: the Light, a realm of goodness, peace and knowledge ruled by the Father of Greatness, and the Darkness, a realm of matter, desire and chaos. The Darkness attacked the Light, and in the struggle that followed particles of light were swallowed and imprisoned in matter. The visible world, in Manichaean myth, was made from the mixture of the two, as a vast machine for gradually freeing the trapped light; the sun and moon are vessels that carry the freed light back to its home.</p><p>Human beings are part of this mixture: their bodies belong to darkness, but their souls are particles of light, forgetful of their origin. Salvation comes through gnosis, knowledge of one’s true nature and of the history of the cosmos, brought by a series of divine messengers: the Living Spirit, the Third Messenger, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> of light, and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>. The history of the world unfolds in three times: the past, when light and darkness were separate; the present, the time of mixture; and the future, when the light will be fully released and the darkness forever confined.</p><p>Manichaean ethics aimed to avoid harming the light in the world. The Elect, the full members of the religion, kept “three seals”: of the mouth, abstaining from meat, wine and lies; of the hands, avoiding work that harms plants, animals or the soil; and of the breast, abstaining from sex. They ate a strictly vegetarian diet, and their digestion of plants was believed to release the light within them.</p><p>The Hearers, lay believers, could marry, work and own property, but they served the Elect, prepared their food and confessed their sins weekly; they hoped to be reborn as Elect in a future life. The great annual festival, the Bema, commemorated Mani’s death before an empty throne representing his presence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medinet-madi.jpg" alt="Medinet Madi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Madi_05.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Einsamer Schütze, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, unlike most founders of religions, wrote his own scriptures, so that his teaching would not be distorted. Manichaean tradition names a canon of seven works written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, a dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>: the Living Gospel, the Treasure of Life, the Pragmateia, the Book of Mysteries, the Book of Giants, the Letters and the Psalms and Prayers. He also wrote the Shabuhragan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, and he illustrated his teaching in a Picture Book, the Ardahang, for Mani was also a painter, remembered in later Persian tradition as a great artist.</p><p>Almost all of these works were lost as the religion was persecuted. For centuries Manichaeism was known mainly through the attacks of its enemies, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, who had been a Hearer for about nine years before becoming a Christian and wrote many works against it.</p><p>In the 20th century spectacular finds transformed knowledge of the religion. German expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, from 1902, recovered thousands of fragments of Manichaean texts in Middle Persian, Parthian, Sogdian and Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>, together with illuminated pages of exquisite book painting. In 1930 a hoard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> codices in Coptic was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, among them the Kephalaia, discourses of Mani, and a Psalm-book. In the 1960s a tiny Greek parchment book of the 5th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> Mani Codex, was identified, containing an account of Mani’s early life. Excavations at Kellis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakhla">Dakhla</a> Oasis of Egypt from the 1990s brought to light the letters and texts of a 4th-century Manichaean community. Chinese Manichaean texts, among them a hymn scroll, were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</p><p>These sources show a religion that translated itself freely into the languages and symbols of each culture it met: Christian in the West, Zoroastrian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Buddhist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where Mani appears as the Buddha of Light.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turfan.jpg" alt="Turfan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-jiaohe-ruinas-d16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manichaeism spread rapidly in both directions from its home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>’s missionaries reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> within his lifetime, and the religion took root across the Roman East, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Roman authorities regarded it with suspicion as a Persian creed: around 302 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> ordered its followers to be burned with their books, and Christian emperors from the late 4th century outlawed it again and again. In the West it was largely extinct by the 6th century, though its name lived on as a charge against later heretics.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> it was persecuted by the Zoroastrian establishment but survived, and its leaders kept their seat in Mesopotamia into the Islamic period. Under the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, Manichaeans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> were repeatedly persecuted as zindiqs, heretics, in the late 8th century, and the leadership moved east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>The religion found its greatest success in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Sogdian merchants carried it along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and in 762 or 763 the ruler of the Uyghur Khaganate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan">Bögü Qaghan</a>, adopted it after meeting Manichaean teachers during a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, making it for a time the religion of a steppe empire. After the fall of the khaganate in 840, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> kingdoms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> oasis continued to patronise Manichaeism alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> into the 10th and 11th centuries.</p><p>In China the religion was tolerated under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, partly as a favour to the Uyghur allies, but in 843 the emperor Wuzong banned it, and in the persecution that followed it lost its public place. It survived in southern China, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, as a secret or semi-Buddhist religion, known as the Religion of Light, into the Ming period; the shrine at Cao’an near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, with its image of Mani carved in 1339, remains. After the 16th century the last traces of the religion fade from the record.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan.jpg" alt="Bögü Qaghan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan">Bögü Qaghan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conversion_of_B%C3%B6g%C3%BC_Qaghan_(759-780_CE)_to_Manicheism_in_762_(detailed_of_B%C3%B6g%C3%BC_Qaghan_in_a_suit_of_armour,_kneeling_to_a_Manichean_high_priest).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8th century Manichean painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Manichaean church was organised with a strict hierarchy modelled on the number twelve. At its head stood a single leader, the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, who resided in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; below him were twelve teachers, seventy-two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and 360 elders, and then the body of the Elect and the much larger body of Hearers.</p><p>The division between the Elect and the Hearers defined Manichaean life. The Elect, men and women, lived in poverty and celibacy, owned nothing beyond food for a day and clothing for a year, travelled to preach and spent their days in prayer, fasting, singing and copying books. They depended on the Hearers for all their needs, and the ritual meal at which the Elect ate the food offered by the Hearers, releasing the light within it, was the central act of Manichaean worship.</p><p>The Hearers lived ordinary lives as merchants, farmers or officials. They were required to keep ten commandments, among them prohibitions on idolatry, lying, killing and adultery, to pray several times a day facing the sun or moon, to fast on certain days and to give alms. Merchants were especially prominent among them, and Manichaean communities followed the trade routes.</p><p>Books and their beauty were central to Manichaeism. Its scribes produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of great refinement, written in a distinctive script derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and illuminated with gold, lapis and brilliant colours, and its hymns and psalms formed a rich devotional literature.</p><p>Because the religion was so often persecuted, its communities frequently lived in secrecy and adapted outwardly to the dominant religion around them, presenting themselves as Christians in the Roman world and as Buddhists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo.jpg" alt="Augustine of Hippo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe de Champaigne, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manichaeism was one of the most widespread religions of the late ancient and early medieval world, stretching at its height from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. No other religion that spread so far has disappeared so completely.</p><p>Its most lasting influence came through its opponents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, who spent nearly a decade as a Manichaean Hearer, developed his account of evil as the absence of good, and his emphasis on human will and divine grace, in conscious opposition to Manichaean dualism, and through him the encounter shaped Western Christian thought. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Christian writers called later dualist movements, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, “Manichaeans”, though historians doubt any direct descent.</p><p>The word “Manichaean” has survived in European languages to describe any world view that divides everything into absolute good and evil.</p><p>The rediscovery of Manichaean texts and art in the 20th century, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-madi">Medinet Madi</a>, Kellis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, revealed a religion of remarkable adaptability and literary and artistic richness. Its book painting is among the earliest surviving illuminated art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>’s vision of a single faith translated into every language anticipated the ambitions of later universal religions.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Manichaeism, founded by the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> in Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around 240, taught a cosmic struggle between light and darkness in which souls are sparks of light to be freed through knowledge and discipline. Spread from the Roman West to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by missionaries and merchants, and persecuted everywhere, it vanished by the early modern period, known again only through texts recovered in the 20th century.</em></p>

